Let \(x^*\) be an unconstrained local minimum of \(f\colon \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R}\) and assume \(f\) is continuously differentiable (\(C^1\)) in an open set containing \(x^*\). Then
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \(\nabla f(x^*) = 0\) (First-Order Necessary Condition)
    \item If in addition \(f\) is twice continuously differentiable in an open set around \(x^*\), then \(\nabla^2 f(x^*) \succeq 0\). (Second Order Necessary Condition)
\end{enumerate}

nl_proof:
Recall the two steps update rule of projected gradient descent
\begin{align*}
y_{s+1} &= x_{s} - \eta_{s}\nabla f(x_{s})\\
x_{s+1} &= \Pi_{\Omega}(y_{s+1})
\end{align*}
First, the proof begins by exploring an upper bound of difference between function values \(f(x_{s})\) and \(f(x^{*})\).
\begin{align*}
f(x_{s}) - f(x^{*}) &\leqslant \nabla f(x_{s})^{\top}(x_{s}-x^{*}) - \frac{\alpha}{2}\|x_{s}-x^{*}\|^{2}\\
			 &=\frac{1}{\eta_{s}} (x_{s} - y_{s+1})^{\top}(x_{s}-x^{*}) - \frac{\alpha}{2}\|x_{s}-x^{*}\|^{2} \tag{by update rule}\\
			 &=\frac{1}{2\eta_{s}} (\|x_{s} - x^{*}\|^{2} + \|x_{s} - y_{s+1}\|^{2} - \|y_{s+1}-x^{*}\|^{2}) - \frac{\alpha}{2}\|x_{s}-x^{*}\|^{2} \tag{by "Fundamental Theorem of Optimization"}\\
			 &=\frac{1}{2\eta_{s}} (\|x_{s} - x^{*}\|^{2} - \|y_{s+1}-x^{*}\|^{2}) +\frac{\eta_{s}}{2}\|\nabla f(x_{s})\|^{2} - \frac{\alpha}{2}\|x_{s}-x^{*}\|^{2} \tag{by update rule}\\
			 &\leqslant \frac{1}{2\eta_{s}} (\|x_{s} - x^{*}\|^{2} - \|x_{s+1}-x^{*}\|^{2}) +\frac{\eta_{s}}{2}\|\nabla f(x_{s})\|^{2} - \frac{\alpha}{2}\|x_{s}-x^{*}\|^{2} \tag{by Pythagorean lemma}\\
			 &\leqslant (\frac{1}{2\eta_{s}} - \frac{\alpha}{2})\|x_{s}-x^{*}\|^{2} -\frac{1}{2\eta_{s}}\|x_{s+1}-x^{*}\|^{2} + \frac{\eta_{s}L^{2}}{2} \tag{by Lipschitzness}
\end{align*}
By multiplying \(s\) on both sides and substituting the step size \(\eta_{s}\) by \(\frac{2}{\alpha(s+1)}\), we get
\[
s(f(x_{s}) - f(x^{*})) \leqslant \frac{L^{2}}{\alpha} + \frac{\alpha}{4}(s(s-1)\|x_{s}-x^{*}\|^{2} - s(s+1)\|x_{s+1} - x^{*}\|^{2})
\]
Finally, we can find the upper bound of the function value obtained using \(t\) steps projected gradient descent
\begin{align*}
f\left(\sum_{s=1}^{t}\frac{2s}{t(t+1)}x_{s}\right)
&\leqslant \sum_{s=1}^{t}\frac{2s}{t(t+1)}f(x_{s})
\tag{by Jensen's inequality}\\
&\leqslant \frac{2}{t(t+1)}\sum_{s=1}^{t}\left(sf(x^{*}) + \frac{L^{2}}{\alpha}
+  \frac{\alpha}{4}(s(s-1)\|x_{s}-x^{*}\|^{2} - s(s+1)\|x_{s+1} - x^{*}\|^{2}) \right)\\
&= \frac{2}{t(t+1)}\sum_{s=1}^{t}sf(x^{*}) + \frac{2L^{2}}{\alpha(t+1)} - \frac{\alpha}{2}\|x_{t+1} - x^{*}\|^{2} \tag{by telescoping sum}\\
&\leqslant f(x^{*}) + \frac{2L^{2}}{\alpha(t+1)}
\end{align*}
This concludes that solving an optimization problem with a strongly convex objective function with projected gradient descent has a convergence rate of the order \(\frac{1}{t+1}\), which is faster compared to the case purely with Lipschitzness.
